--TEST--
`Core\Log::write` renders each record as one JSON Lines object carrying its level's own lowercase name, whatever the message contains -- `rule:errors/log-write` and `rule:errors/log-level`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:errors/log-level`'s five cases, loudest first, which is also the order their
// syslog severities run in. What the record carries is the case's own name
// lowercased, never the number it is valued by -- the severity is what a
// `syslog` target needs and a JSON Lines target has no use for.
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Critical, "the floor");
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Error, "an operation failed");
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Warn, "a retry");
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Info, "started");
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Debug, "detail");

// Each line names where it was written, by
// `rule:errors/a-record-names-where-it-was-produced`: the file as the program
// named it and the statement's own one-based line, with no `member` key at all
// at file scope rather than an empty one.

// `rule:errors/log-write` chose JSON over logfmt for exactly this: whatever a message
// wanted to be, a record is one line, and the quoting is the encoder's rather
// than a judgement made at the site that writes it.
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Info, "a \"quoted\" word\nand a second line");
--EXPECT--
{"level":"critical","msg":"the floor","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":6}}
{"level":"error","msg":"an operation failed","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":7}}
{"level":"warn","msg":"a retry","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":8}}
{"level":"info","msg":"started","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":9}}
{"level":"debug","msg":"detail","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":10}}
{"level":"info","msg":"a \"quoted\" word\nand a second line","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":20}}
